WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:37.500
With an initial exchange rate of USD 1.1789,
January 1st of 1999 has gone down in history

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:49.240
as the day the euro was born.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:57.659
It began to operate as an electronic currency,
used in financial markets and for cashless

00:00:57.659 --> 00:00:58.659
payments.

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:05.640
However, it wouldn’t be until three years
later, that is the beginning of 2002, when

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.509
the Euro coins and notes that we all know
today began to circulate.

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:14.500
It had all started a decade earlier, on January
1st, 1991 when 11 member countries of the

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.740
European Union decided to integrate their
local currencies – currencies such as the

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:23.280
mark, franc, lira or peseta – under the
supervising umbrella of the European Central

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.760
Bank.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:44.560
The Euro era had begun.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.450
And it did so in style.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.060
Expectations were huge.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.780
Perhaps no one showed more confidence in this
new currency than the then European Commissioner

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.390
for Monetary Affairs, Yves-Thibault.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:31.359
Since then, 20 years have passed, a time in

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:35.599
which the new European currency has gone through
both good and bad times...

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:40.450
Which means, on many occasions, the euro has
been the main topic of public debate.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:46.819
Because, folks, we can’t deny it, the news
is notoriously pessimistic.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:51.739
Catastrophes and bad news draw more attention
than positive events.

00:02:51.739 --> 00:02:57.639
It’s easy to believe that the latest news
supercedes any slow and steady trend.

00:02:57.639 --> 00:03:01.989
So, in recent years we have been hearing all
kinds of theories about what the euro has

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:05.019
meant and forecasts about this currency’s
future.

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:08.719
Surely, you must’ve heard at some point
that the euro is doomed or that its arrival

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:11.769
has led to winning and losing countries in
the eurozone.

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:13.349
But what’s the truth?

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:18.069
What assessment can we make of the 20 years
that the euro has been part of most Europeans’

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.349
daily lives?

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:26.129
What challenges does it face?

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:32.189
Well, to answer these and other questions
and to celebrate the 20th anniversary of the

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:51.609
European currency, thanks to the support and
sponsorship of the European

00:03:51.609 --> 00:04:10.530
Central Bank, some of VisualPolitik’s team
members traveled to Frankfurt.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:15.079
In this video, we’re going to try and find
out what the euro has meant and what challenges

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:17.370
it faces.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:23.830
So, let’s go.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:30.699
(UNITY IS STRENGTH)
I think the first question we need to ask

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:34.690
ourselves is quite clear, how and why did
the euro emerge?

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.240
To answer this question, we need to go back
to the 80s, a decade that wasn’t particularly

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.820
good for Europeans.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.000
At that time, Europe’s economy wasn’t
having a great time.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.310
These were years of stagnation and bad statistics.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:50.310
For example...

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.620
Unemployment in the European Community’s
countries had practically doubled, going from

00:04:53.620 --> 00:05:02.789
5.8% in 1980 to over 11% just five years later;
Potential growth had decreased by three points;

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:05.580
Public deficits had begun to emerge with levels
above 3%

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:13.770
And to make matters worse, the United States
had tripled their imports subjected to trade

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:14.770
restrictions.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:21.919
Well, it was in this context that the idea
of building a true common market that would

00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:27.330
allow European nations to boost trade and,
subsequently, strengthen their economic growth.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.729
The idea was to create a market without barriers,
without customs, without tariffs, without

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:33.419
restrictions for all goods.

00:05:33.419 --> 00:05:37.939
To do so, all administrative obstacles would
have to be eliminated and the disparity between

00:05:37.939 --> 00:05:40.270
rules and regulations ended.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:47.189
If a product could be sold in one country
of the union, it could be sold in any of them.

00:05:47.189 --> 00:05:49.789
But... not only that.

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:55.180
A genuine common market had to work with the
same currency and with the same monetary policy.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.060
Among other things, this would prevent countries
competing with each other by devaluing their

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.409
respective currencies over and over again.

00:06:01.409 --> 00:06:03.440
And that was quite common in those times.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:07:05.050
For example, between 1979 and 1992 alone,
Italy devalued the lira seven times.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:14.840
In just 13 years, seven devaluations!

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:38.449
And what’s more, setting a common currency
would make trade between the European countries

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:39.620
much, much easier.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.340
Companies would no longer have to look at
exchange rates, monetary policy decisions

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:49.319
from different governments or currency risk
hedges, among many other issues.

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:55.479
And, you know what?

00:07:55.479 --> 00:08:00.780
The creation of the single market has been
a clear example of success.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.540
It fulfilled its objective.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:09.439
Intra-Community exports have moved from representing
13% of the European Union's GDP in 1992 to

00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:10.599
more than 20% today.

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:17.740
And the volume of intra-community investments
has also skyrocketed.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:30.490
And, in all honesty, throughout this entire
process the euro has played a key role.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:33.010
The euro has now been with us for 20 years.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.110
What conclusion can we make?

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:42.180
Has it been good or bad for Europeans?

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:55.130
Let’s take a look.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:09:02.920
Folks, Europe has changed a lot in the last
20 years, in some cases for good and in others...

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.420
let’s say not so good.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:06.900
But at this point, let me ask you a question,
yes, yes, to you.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:07.900
What do you think?

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.780
Has the euro been good or bad for Europe?

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:14.310
Well, folks, with the data at hand, I think
we can say that, in general, its influence

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.310
has been very positive.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:17.820
We’ve already talked about it here on VisualPolitik.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:10:49.410
Now, the question is, how positive?

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:58.410
Let's put some numbers on the table
Nowadays, more than 340 million people use

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:04.190
the euro and it has replaced national currencies
in 19 of the 28 states that make up the European

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.490
Union.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:17.290
Since its inception, the euro zone’s GDP
per capita has increased by 52%, going from

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:23.260
about 21,000 to more than 32,000 € and today
the work participation rate is higher in the

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:27.510
euro area than in the United States.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:32.160
In spite of that, inflation has reached an
average of 1.7%, a rate lower than the ones

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.200
recorded in the seventies, eighties or nineties.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:38.240
In fact, the eurozone’s inflation level
in these twenty years has been lower than

00:11:38.240 --> 00:12:25.390
that of the Federal Republic of Germany over
the same period of time.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:27.770
This might explain the euro’s huge popularity.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:31.240
Actually only 20% of Europeans are against
it.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.060
According to the latest surveys, three out
of every four European citizens have a good

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.310
opinion of the currency and think that it
has been positive.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:41.670
[And let's not kid ourselves, it isn’t strange
to think so.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:45.180
I’m sure that if you were offered to have
your savings in euros or pesetas – or in

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.850
some currency that exists today and resembles
peseta – you wouldn’t think it twice.]

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:52.010
But hold on a second, because not everything
has been sunshine.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:59.180
In the last 20 years the euro has also faced
some very hard times.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:12.240
Between 2008 and 2012 things became very difficult.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:16.940
The global financial crisis caused a lot of
problems in most Western economies, and true

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:21.140
nightmares in some of the countries using
the euro, such as Greece, Ireland, Italy,

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:22.140
Spain or Portugal.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:27.970
Clearly, that’s when the idea of the two
Europes emerged.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:32.150
On the one hand were central and northern
European countries, countries that have generally

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:37.520
high productivity indexes, competitive industries
and relatively responsible leaderships.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:43.640
And on the other, countries that had historically
attempted to compete by devaluing their currencies.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:47.550
When this option was ruled out due to the
euro, they had boosted domestic demand through

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:50.530
strong imbalances in their payments balance.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:57.720
So to understand this better, with funding
from abroad, basically from northern and central

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:58.990
European countries.

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:04.490
Financing that had been largely concentrated
in the real estate sector.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.250
We’re talking about countries where the
local governments didn’t implement any structural

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:12.680
reforms that would allow them to be more competitive,
and whose banks were overloaded with loans

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.190
linked to the real estate bubble...

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:18.480
Loans that of course lost a lot of their value
when this bubble burst, which, obviously,

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.850
put the banks against the wall.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:29.780
Greece, Portugal, Spain and Ireland – although
Ireland’s case is a bit different – required

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:31.660
some form of financial assistance.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:35.871
At that time, the distrust and problems were
so huge, that, as you might have heard, there

00:14:35.871 --> 00:14:39.280
was speculation about the possible rupture
of the euro.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:43.170
Among other things because many European states
had to pay such high risk premiums that their

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.420
situation was made completely unsustainable.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:51.530
They could not finance themselves.

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:55.730
In the end, the member countries of the Eurozone
decided to do everything they could to avoid

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:00.680
that scenario and it was then that European
Central Bank President, Mario Draghi, pronounced

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:16.870
these famous words, on July 26th, 2012:

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:20.740
This meant that the European Central Bank
would adopt extraordinary measures to stabilise

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:21.740
the euro.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:25.860
It started to give longer term loans to banks
or buy a range of assets from banks including

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.950
government bonds and corporate bonds, so that
businesses and people were able to borrow

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:33.010
more and spend less to repay their debts.

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:37.630
This has led to more economic growth.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:16:00.400
But, folks, at this point the question we
need to ask ourselves is, is the eurozone

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:03.890
really prepared to face a new crisis?

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:14.390
What could happen in the future if one of
the euro member countries has problems?

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:20.570
Folks, over the last few years a lot of different
mechanisms have emerged and been implemented

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.240
to strengthen the euro’s resistance.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:31.670
And that’s not all, existing institutions
such as The Stability and Growth Pact have

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:35.020
also been reinforced.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:18:41.750
They/it requires a 3% limit on public deficit
and 60% on public debt from

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:47.420
member countries.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:19:33.530
("Within our mandate, the ECB is ready to
do whatever it takes to preserve the euro.

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:57.280
And believe
me, it will be enough”.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.860
Mario Draghi)

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:13.930
And also 
by virtue of the Fiscal Stability Treaty,

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:18.470
almost all European Union countries have included
the requirement of having balanced budgets

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:20.800
throughout the cycle in their national legislation.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:25.290
[Let's hope they respect their legislation]
The European Central Bank has also increased

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:29.990
its capabilities to deal with emergencies.

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:39.140
In short, as you see a lot of changes have
taken place with the aim of reducing the risk

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.940
of crisis and, if necessary, limiting its
impact.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:46.309
The question is, are these measures enough
or should we continue to move forward, for

00:20:46.309 --> 00:20:48.230
example with a fiscal union?

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:53.170
Right there, folks, is one of the great debates
that exist right now in Europe.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:21:01.060
But, just a second, crises aren’t the only
challenges facing the European Central Bank

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:02.060
and the euro.

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:05.850
The arrival of cryptocurrency and blockchain
have also raised questions:

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:09.010
Will new financial technologies be able to
change the way money works?

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:11.890
Do they pose a threat to traditional currencies?

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:16.380
And this brings us to another very important
question: What about cybersecurity?

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.760
Especially in a digital world.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:38.990
Folks, Europe is facing a lot of challenges,
some of which we

00:21:38.990 --> 00:23:18.400
have

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:22.690
already talked about on this channel.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:26.660
Challenges such as aging populations, growing
international competition, imbalances in the

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:32.190
public accounts of many of the member countries
or even defining exactly what European model

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:38.270
we all want to follow.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:44.390
The European Parliament elections will take
place on May 26th.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:49.680
In one way or another, this is the moment
in which all European citizens will have the

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:54.140
chance to raise our voices and decide what
future we want for Europe or what kind of

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:55.900
union we want.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:56.900
So...

00:23:56.900 --> 00:23:57.900
Now you know.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:01.390
In the meantime, if you found this video interesting,
give us a hand by liking it.

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:05.001
And once again, many thanks to the European
Central Bank for all their help making this

00:24:05.001 --> 00:24:06.001
video.

00:24:06.001 --> 00:24:20.740
And as always, thank you 
for watching.

